WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.278 --> 00:00:03.856
Some decisions are routine and contained. Others can change

2
00:00:03.878 --> 00:00:07.678
a policy, service, budget, opportunity or process for many

3
00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:11.322
people. Equity analysis should be useful at either scale,

4
00:00:11.611 --> 00:00:13.833
but the amount of review and the people involved

5
00:00:13.856 --> 00:00:14.500
can differ.

6
00:00:15.267 --> 00:00:19.089
Begin by checking the current DHS equity policy, the

7
00:00:19.111 --> 00:00:22.333
official Equity Analysis Toolkit and the procedures that apply

8
00:00:22.356 --> 00:00:25.956
to your work. The published DHS policy requires a

9
00:00:25.978 --> 00:00:29.111
formal equity analysis for actions with prolonged or long-term

10
00:00:29.133 --> 00:00:32.044
impact that are scheduled for approval by a supervisor,

11
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:36.000
manager, director, assistant or deputy commissioner, or commissioner.

12
00:00:36.744 --> 00:00:40.011
The policy says those actions must be accompanied by

13
00:00:40.033 --> 00:00:43.856
the DHS Equity Analysis or DHS Equity Scan in

14
00:00:43.878 --> 00:00:47.233
the toolkit, or a customized tool developed in consultation

15
00:00:47.256 --> 00:00:50.944
with an equity director. Confirm the current policy and

16
00:00:50.967 --> 00:00:52.922
user guide with the responsible office.

17
00:00:53.511 --> 00:00:56.911
The brief Equity Pause in this application is an

18
00:00:56.933 --> 00:00:59.844
additional learning aid; do not assume it is the

19
00:00:59.867 --> 00:01:03.378
official DHS Equity Scan or that it satisfies a

20
00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:04.400
required process.

21
00:01:06.492 --> 00:01:10.070
If a full analysis or another review is required,

22
00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:13.670
follow that requirement. A quick check does not replace

23
00:01:13.692 --> 00:01:16.870
a required full analysis, and a short deadline does

24
00:01:16.892 --> 00:01:19.314
not by itself decide what is sufficient.

25
00:01:20.127 --> 00:01:22.949
The choices in this lesson help you think about

26
00:01:22.971 --> 00:01:25.882
a starting point. They are not a separate approval

27
00:01:25.904 --> 00:01:29.838
rule. When the right path is unclear, identify the

28
00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:33.238
question and ask the responsible person or office.

29
00:01:34.434 --> 00:01:38.034
An Equity Pause is a brief way to examine

30
00:01:38.057 --> 00:01:41.679
a routine decision before it is settled. You might

31
00:01:41.701 --> 00:01:45.257
ask who could benefit, who could face an obstacle,

32
00:01:45.634 --> 00:01:48.301
what you know, what you do not know and

33
00:01:48.323 --> 00:01:50.146
whether someone else should be involved.

34
00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:55.009
For example, a team changing the time of an

35
00:01:55.031 --> 00:01:58.076
internal meeting might use a short check to consider

36
00:01:58.098 --> 00:02:02.831
access, schedules and participation. If a barrier appears, the

37
00:02:02.853 --> 00:02:06.742
response may require more inquiry or another person's review.

38
00:02:07.409 --> 00:02:09.498
The value of the pause is the question it

39
00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:12.187
opens, not the speed with which a form is

40
00:02:12.209 --> 00:02:12.631
finished.

41
00:02:14.679 --> 00:02:17.923
A brief check does not establish that every person

42
00:02:17.946 --> 00:02:20.501
has been reached or that a change is equitable.

43
00:02:21.368 --> 00:02:23.834
It also does not authorize someone to make a

44
00:02:23.857 --> 00:02:28.368
decision outside their responsibilities. Use the appropriate process when

45
00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:31.523
a requirement or an emerging concern calls for more.

46
00:02:32.576 --> 00:02:35.642
An analysis gives you room to examine the decision,

47
00:02:35.931 --> 00:02:41.242
the people affected, evidence, alternatives, participation, implementation and follow-up.

48
00:02:41.731 --> 00:02:44.176
It is especially useful when the proposed change could

49
00:02:44.198 --> 00:02:47.464
alter how people reach information, services or opportunities.

50
00:02:48.364 --> 00:02:52.387
Return to the reminder example. Replacing mailed reminders with

51
00:02:52.409 --> 00:02:55.053
text-only messages could change the way people receive information

52
00:02:55.076 --> 00:02:59.187
and act on an appointment. Before choosing a direction,

53
00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:02.809
the team needs to understand the applicable requirements, seek

54
00:03:02.831 --> 00:03:06.876
relevant experience and compare options. The official tool and

55
00:03:06.898 --> 00:03:09.520
responsible reviewers guide what the work requires.

56
00:03:10.464 --> 00:03:13.376
An analysis does not have to begin with a

57
00:03:13.398 --> 00:03:16.887
perfect data set. It can identify what is known,

58
00:03:17.131 --> 00:03:20.198
what is uncertain and how to learn more. Be

59
00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:23.820
honest about those limits, particularly when an alternative could

60
00:03:23.842 --> 00:03:26.331
create a new burden while removing another.

61
00:03:27.348 --> 00:03:31.148
Sometimes a concern points beyond the immediate decision. A

62
00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:33.437
reminder may be only one part of an appointment

63
00:03:33.459 --> 00:03:38.926
process that also includes scheduling, language support, transportation information,

64
00:03:39.014 --> 00:03:41.726
rescheduling and follow-up. If an obstacle appears across several

65
00:03:41.748 --> 00:03:44.459
steps, a more sustained review may be needed.

66
00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:48.314
That review can involve the people affected and the

67
00:03:48.337 --> 00:03:52.226
offices responsible for the process while meaningful choices remain

68
00:03:52.248 --> 00:03:55.426
open. A staff member can help by describing what

69
00:03:55.448 --> 00:03:58.914
they observed, bringing forward evidence and identifying where the

70
00:03:58.937 --> 00:04:01.648
question needs to go. They need not claim authority

71
00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:03.137
over the entire process.

72
00:04:03.903 --> 00:04:06.970
More review is not automatically better if it delays

73
00:04:06.992 --> 00:04:10.526
an urgent response without purpose. Follow any applicable immediate

74
00:04:10.548 --> 00:04:14.014
procedure, address what you are authorized to address, and

75
00:04:14.037 --> 00:04:16.948
identify the appropriate review for the broader question.

76
00:04:18.092 --> 00:04:21.403
In the activity below, consider the proposed text-only reminder

77
00:04:21.426 --> 00:04:25.270
change. Choose the most responsible next step when you

78
00:04:25.292 --> 00:04:28.314
do not yet know the applicable review requirement or

79
00:04:28.337 --> 00:04:32.070
how people would experience the change. After choosing, read

80
00:04:32.092 --> 00:04:34.559
the feedback. You can compare all three responses.

81
00:04:35.423 --> 00:04:38.601
The practice is about preparing for a decision, not

82
00:04:38.623 --> 00:04:42.046
declaring a final result. Do not enter information about

83
00:04:42.068 --> 00:04:44.334
an actual person's appointment or services.

84
00:04:45.701 --> 00:04:49.212
For your own example, note the proposed change, who

85
00:04:49.234 --> 00:04:53.079
may be affected, the likely consequences if access differs,

86
00:04:53.368 --> 00:04:56.123
and the policy or procedure you need to check.

87
00:04:56.812 --> 00:04:59.412
Identify what you know, what you need to confirm

88
00:04:59.568 --> 00:05:02.168
and who can help determine the required review.

89
00:05:02.797 --> 00:05:06.063
The existing analysis tools offer a quick check, a

90
00:05:06.086 --> 00:05:09.530
standard analysis and an in-depth review. Their labels help

91
00:05:09.552 --> 00:05:13.752
you find support; current requirements and responsible review determine

92
00:05:13.774 --> 00:05:16.574
what your work must include. In the next lesson,

93
00:05:16.597 --> 00:05:19.419
we will make those requirements and decision roles visible

94
00:05:19.441 --> 00:05:20.797
before comparing options.
